          "input": "text(await tools.apply_patch(\"*** Begin Patch\\n*** Add File: /tmp/harness-okf-assessment.md\\n+# Assessment: OKF for harness references\\n+\\n+Adopt a minimal OKF bundle when splitting the catalog into per-project files. Moderate confidence in adopting OKF; high confidence that selective loading is the immediate benefit. Adding frontmatter to the existing monolith would provide little practical value.\\n+\\n+## Findings\\n+\\n+- F1: The current catalog contains 25 references in a 57 KB document. `AGENTS.md:16` eagerly imports it; `CLAUDE.local.md:5` also references it with an eager import. Its introduction and ranked index occupy approximately 4 KB. Loading only that entrypoint would reduce this document's initial byte contribution by roughly 93%. This is a file-size comparison, not a measured token or task-performance improvement.\\n+- F2: Existing entries already contain source snapshots, precise source links, applicability judgments, and cautions. OKF would make those records more uniform and individually addressable. It would not improve their factual accuracy or justify repeating the research.\\n+- F3: OKF v0.2 supports this use directly: `Reference` is a documented type, only `type` is mandatory, and `index.md` supports progressive disclosure. Authorship, verification, and expiry are optional. See [concept documents](https://github.com/GoogleCloudPlatform/open-knowledge-format/blob/ad30107c31c06aec8a7d5636e0d1058118604e6f/SPEC.md#4-concept-documents) and [index files](https://github.com/GoogleCloudPlatform/open-knowledge-format/blob/ad30107c31c06aec8a7d5636e0d1058118604e6f/SPEC.md#8-index-files).\\n+- F4: The incremental return beyond ordinary Markdown files is a consistent metadata and citation contract. Future interoperability is plausible; this assessment establishes no current llame runtime consumer that would automatically enforce OKF trust or freshness fields.\\n+\\n+## Options\\n+\\n+| Option | Effect | Judgment |\\n+| --- | --- | --- |\\n+| O1: Keep the monolith | No migration cost; entire catalog stays in initial context | Weak fit for a growing reference collection |\\n+| O2: Wrap the monolith in OKF metadata | Bundle-level metadata; same loading and maintenance granularity | Low return |\\n+| O3: One minimal OKF document per project | Selective reads, independent updates, standard metadata | Recommended |\\n+\\n+## Proposed scope\\n+\\n+Use `docs/research/harnesses/` as the bundle root. It currently contains only the catalog. Replace the catalog with one `index.md` and 25 project files. Keep the current ranking, OpenClaw's explicit alpha priority, and llame's specification authority in the index. Keep evidence and cautions in the individual references.\\n+\\n+Each project gets `type: Reference`, `title`, a concise `description`, the upstream `resource`, and `sources` for cited artifacts. Preserve exact source revisions and line anchors. Use OKF's keyed source footnotes for claim attribution while retaining readable, clickable evidence. Keep observed dates and revisions distinct from the date the document was generated. Avoid adding custom fields unless a concrete consumer needs them.\\n+\\n+Record `generated` only with accurate authorship and time. Add `verified` only for an actual review against sources, with its scope clear in the prose; approval of the format is not factual human verification. Defer `stale_after` until there is a deliberate refresh policy. It is a declared expiry, not upstream change detection. These distinctions follow [OKF provenance, trust, and lifecycle metadata](https://github.com/GoogleCloudPlatform/open-knowledge-format/blob/ad30107c31c06aec8a7d5636e0d1058118604e6f/SPEC.md#5-provenance-trust-and-lifecycle).\\n+\\n+Update the imports and links in `AGENTS.md`, `README.md`, and the local `CLAUDE.local.md`. Keep only the small index eagerly loaded and direct agents to read individual references as needed. Maintain one ranked index. Git already records change history